Adding SLF4J logging to a Java class is a three-step process.

First, add the dependency. In a Spring Boot project, SLF4J and Logback are already included transitively through spring-boot-starter, so no extra dependency is needed.

Second, declare the logger. At the top of your class, declare a private static final Logger field:
```java
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;

public class OrderService {
    private static final Logger logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(OrderService.class);
    // ...
}
```

The logger should be `private static final` — private because it's internal, static because it's shared by all instances of the class (no need to create a new logger per instance), and final because it should never be reassigned.

Third, write log statements. Use the appropriate level method and use `{}` placeholders for variables — never concatenate strings:
```java
logger.info("Processing order {} for customer {}", orderId, customerId);
logger.warn("Order {} is delayed by {} minutes", orderId, delay);
logger.error("Failed to process order {}", orderId, exception);
```

Using `{}` placeholders is important because SLF4J only evaluates them and converts the variables to strings if that log level is enabled. String concatenation always happens, even when the log level is off, which wastes CPU.

In Spring Boot projects using Lombok, you can skip the logger declaration entirely by adding the `@Slf4j` annotation to the class, and Lombok will generate the logger field automatically. This is very common in modern Spring Boot codebases.
